      "title": "Computer History Museum · Apple iMac poster",
      "url": "https://www.computerhistory.org/revolution/artifact/1215",
      "kind": "Dated original advertising object",
      "finding": "A 1998 Apple poster provides a physical-product reference for colourful translucent computing.",
      "checked": "2026-10-29"
    },
    {
      "id": "S22",
      "title": "Swiss Camera Museum · The digital revolution",
      "url": "https://www.cameramuseum.ch/en/discover/permanent-exhibition/the-digital-revolution/",
      "kind": "Museum technology history",
      "finding": "The chronology follows scanners, early digital cameras and broader adoption: film and digital coexisted rather than changing overnight.",
      "checked": "2026-10-29"
    },
    {
      "id": "S23",
      "title": "Daptone Records · Charles Bradley",
      "url": "https://daptonerecords.com/artists/charles-bradley/",
      "kind": "Artist’s label / releases",
      "finding": "The label lists the Changes single in 2013 and album in 2016; its biography documents the earlier working network. Release date and revival aesthetic need separate fields.",
      "checked": "2026-10-29"
    },
    {
      "id": "S24",
      "title": "Fisk Jubilee Singers · History",
      "url": "https://www.fiskjubileesingers.org/history",
      "kind": "Ensemble / institutional history",
      "finding": "The student ensemble began touring in 1871. This supports a named concert-ensemble context, rather than generic nineteenth-century racial imagery.",
      "checked": "2026-10-29"
    },
    {
      "id": "S25",
      "title": "Library of Congress · Swing Low, Sweet Chariot",
      "url": "https://www.loc.gov/item/jukebox-128141/",
      "kind": "Exact recording catalogue",
      "finding": "The Victor item is dated 1 December 1909 and identifies the quartet’s members. A later ensemble recording must remain distinct from the 1871 tour.",
      "checked": "2026-10-29"
    },
    {
      "id": "S26",
      "title": "BFI · Film colour systems",
      "url": "https://www.bfi.org.uk/lists/10-best-film-colour-systems",
      "kind": "Film-archive specialist account",
      "finding": "Silent-era tinting, toning and applied colour predate later colour-film systems. Early cinema was not uniformly black and white; saturated colour was not invented in the 1960s.",
      "checked": "2026-10-29"
    },
    {
      "id": "S27",
      "title": "Fraunhofer IIS · 30 years of .mp3",
      "url": "https://www.iis.fraunhofer.de/en/magazin/panorama/2025/30-years-of-mp3.html",
      "kind": "Research institution / first-person technical history",
      "finding": "The account describes Tom’s Diner as a difficult test item in codec development. The proposed visual reference is a listening laboratory, not a dancefloor.",
      "checked": "2026-10-29"
    },
    {
      "id": "S28",
      "title": "V&A · The Music is Black: large-print guide",
      "url": "https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/the-music-is-black-large-print-guide",
      "kind": "Exhibition objects / curatorial account",
      "finding": "Records, equipment, signs, photographs and decorated record boxes ground Jamaican and British sound-system histories in people and objects.",
      "checked": "2026-10-29"
    },
    {
      "id": "S29",
      "title": "CCA Lagos · Fela, Ghariokwu Lemi and album-cover art",
      "url": "https://ccalagos.org/fela-ghariokwu-lemi-and-the-art-of-the-album-cover/",
      "kind": "Local arts institution / exhibition account",
      "finding": "Lemi’s sleeve art combines illustration and political commentary. A named Lagos practice is more informative than a continent-wide decorative pattern.",
      "checked": "2026-10-29"
    },
    {
      "id": "S30",
      "title": "MoMA · Tadanori Yokoo, 1965 poster",
      "url": "https://www.moma.org/collection/works/7953",
      "kind": "Dated silkscreen object",
      "finding": "A 1965 silkscreen anchors a Japanese poster reference. It does not establish a universal Japanese or city-pop style.",
      "checked": "2026-10-29"
    },
    {
      "id": "S31",
      "title": "Whitney · Hélio Oiticica, Tropicália",
      "url": "https://whitney.org/media/2617",
      "kind": "Museum interpretation / audio transcript",
      "finding": "The 1966–67 installation offers a participatory, mixed-material reference linked to a specific Brazilian artist; it is not a template for all Brazilian music.",
      "checked": "2026-10-29"
    },
    {
      "id": "S32",
      "title": "NMAAHC · Afrofuturism",
